{"id":"F619","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"Checked on the page. Solicitation M26-159 (period 1/21/26–1/20/27, due 1 Dec 2025, now closed) lists NovaSeq 6000 A01063 on a Gold plan with 1 PM, NovaSeq X Plus LH00551 on Silver, a 'NextSeq X Plus' LH00236 on Silver, a MiSeq and two DRAGEN servers. 'NextSeq X Plus' is not a real product. The LH serial prefix is a NovaSeq X-series prefix, so this is probably a second X Plus with a typo in the listing. That means the X count does not rely on M25-214, which I did not check. This is one federal research lab (an FFRDC), not a clinical tail site. Dual-running anecdotes are already known, and a service tier does not measure 6000 use. Treat it as an incremental anecdote."},
{"id":"F627","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"The ENA bucket arithmetic is consistent with F644. 'Not decelerating' is only true in share points. In logistic odds the multiple slowed from 3.9x to 2.7x, so conversion is decelerating. Release dates (first_public) lag sequencing by months to years, and SRA is research-heavy, while ~80% of the remaining 6000 Gb is clinical and never deposited. This is not evidence on the clinical tail, which is the subject of 1A. Known D052 already covers the deposit-lag point for GEO."},
{"id":"F628","verdict":"reject","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"Duplicates F644: same endpoint, same quarters, different bins. Keep F644 as the primary item. The 6000 Gb fall comes out at −10% to −13% under either binning, so the direction holds. Also note that ENA's 6000 run count (511,741, +21% y/y) conflicts with known F595 (Entrez SRA, 407,958, flat). The two use different date fields, so neither run series should anchor anything."},
{"id":"F629","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"Consistent with the F644 buckets. A caveat on reading it: SRA base_count is per run accession, which roughly means per library or sample, not per instrument flow cell. 'Runs of 60 Gb or more' therefore means deep samples (WGS-scale), not large flow cells. That is valid for 'genome-scale work is converting', but the data is research-weighted and lagged."},
{"id":"F632","verdict":"reject","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"against","note":"This restates known D052 (public deposits lag Illumina's reported share by about 2 years) for SRA instead of GEO. It is a methodological caveat, not evidence against 1A. Its own extrapolation is also inconsistent: +1.7 pts per quarter from 83% gives about 93% by Q4'27, not 88–89%. The revenue-mix point is already known (each 5 pts of X share ≈ −8.5% of HT revenue)."},
{"id":"F635","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"2B","direction":"against","note":"Verified in the 10x Q2'26 10-Q. The D. Del. suit was filed May 2026 on US 11,021,737 / 11,566,276 / 11,566,277 / 12,264,358 against AVITI24. The UPC Munich suit asserts EP 4249605 and EP 4491741. Element filed a partial motion to dismiss and an EPO opposition against EP '741 in July 2026, and no schedule is set. This is new, since the known list covers only the Illumina–Element suits. The impact on research pricing is speculative: it is early-stage, covers AVITI24 single-cell and spatial only, and an injunction is years away."},
{"id":"F641","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"2B","direction":"against","note":"Plausible and new, but per-accession base_count partly reflects AVITI's lower output per flow cell and different multiplexing, not only shallow experiments. Single-cell is one counting application. This does not show Illumina pricing power."},
{"id":"F644","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"Verified: 6000 Q3'25 total = 421,447, 6000 Q3'26 ≥10 Gb = 54,042, X Q3'26 ≥200 Gb = 713. The bucket sums reconcile exactly to the totals, and my recomputed Gb is 2.535 Pb → 2.199 Pb for the 6000 (−13%) and 0.516 → 1.228 Pb for the X. X Gb share: 16.9% → 35.8%. Caveats: first_public lag, research-weighted, assumed bucket means. Downgrade to weak if used to argue about the clinical tail."},
{"id":"F645","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"The counts are right, but the inference is overstated. Sub-1 Gb 'runs' are small libraries (amplicons, small RNA), usually multiplexed on S4 flow cells. They do not identify low-volume users on small, high-price flow cells. Sub-1 Gb runs also carry only ~4% of 6000 Gb (~97K of 2,199K Gb). See the derived item: in Gb terms the 6000 tail is not turning into a small-run tail."},
{"id":"F646","verdict":"reject","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"against","note":"The run counts duplicate F629/F644. The inference is wrong in Gb terms: 74% of public 6000 Gb in Q3'26 still sits in libraries of 10 Gb or more (77% in Q3'25), and 54K such 6000 runs remain against 26K on the X. The remaining 6000 work is not mainly tiny runs. Run size also does not identify Thaysen's cohort 1, which is clinical and not in SRA."},
{"id":"F648","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"2B","direction":"supports","note":"Verified: DNBSEQ Q3'26 ≥10 Gb = 14,976. The NovaSeq figures reconcile (6000 63,713 + X 12,329 = 76,042; 54,042 + 26,266 = 80,308), and the share math is correct. This extends known F610 (T7 run surge) to large runs. The activity is China- and Asia-weighted with little bearing on US/EU research pricing, and Illumina's Greater China revenue is already −19%. Indirect for 2B."},
{"id":"F649","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"2B","direction":"against","note":"Verified: Element Q3'26 ≥10 Gb = 462. This is a useful correction to known F609/F583 (AVITI run growth). Large-library AVITI activity is flat, ~0.6% of NovaSeq large runs and ~3% of DNBSEQ's. Same caveats on per-accession base_count and lag apply."}
